/**
 * Discriminator for typed prompt payloads.  `debug` is a debugging-discipline variant of the free-form mode: it reuses the free-form payload shape but swaps in a system prompt tuned for methodical root-cause investigation. It is explicit-selection-only — no workflow node emits it and no terminal fallback resolves to it; a user must deliberately choose the debug mode to start a run with it. There is deliberately no `coding_debug` follow-up analogue mirroring `coding_freeform`: debug-session follow-ups already re-ground through the mode-agnostic `coding_freeform` prompt, which re-situates the model in the coding-session context regardless of the prior promptType, so a debug-specific follow-up prompt would be redundant. Note that the `PromptTypeResponseDto` mirror in workflows.yaml is intentionally NOT extended with `debug` (it is selection-only, never API-returned), so the lockstep rule between that mirror and this enum is not misread as requiring the addition there.  `research` is a read-only investigation variant of the free-form mode: it reuses the free-form payload shape but swaps in a system prompt tuned for a read-only investigative posture — gathering evidence and synthesizing findings without mutating code or entities. Like `debug`, it is explicit-selection-only: no workflow node emits it and no terminal fallback resolves to it; a user must deliberately choose the research mode to start a run with it. There is deliberately no `coding_research` follow-up analogue mirroring `coding_freeform`: research-session follow-ups already re-ground through the mode-agnostic `coding_freeform` prompt, which re-situates the model in the coding-session context regardless of the prior promptType, so a research-specific follow-up prompt would be redundant. The `PromptTypeResponseDto` mirror in workflows.yaml is intentionally NOT extended with `research` (it is selection-only, never API-returned), so the lockstep rule between that mirror and this enum is not misread as requiring the addition there.  `quick` is a minimal pass-through variant: no graph fetching, no design-doc loading, no Project Assistant pass. The user\'s message is the full prompt; the handler applies only a brief coding-context framing. Like `debug` and `research`, it is explicit-selection-only — no workflow node emits it and no terminal fallback resolves to it. There is no follow-up analogue; quick sessions re-ground through the mode-agnostic `coding_freeform` prompt on follow-ups. The `PromptTypeResponseDto` mirror in workflows.yaml is intentionally NOT extended with `quick` (selection-only, never API-returned), and `quick` is NOT added to `PROJECT_ASSISTANT_ELIGIBLE_PROMPT_TYPES`.  `coding_freeform` is the override-by-construction system prompt for coding-session follow-ups. When a user sends a free-form message into an active coding session, the message-service edge sets this prompt type so the resolver swaps in a fresh, situating system prompt that re-grounds the model in the coding-session context — naming the session, surfacing the prior promptType as a loose fact, and orienting positively toward the user\'s latest message. This closes the empty-prompt re-grounding hole left when a follow-up turn would otherwise inherit the previous turn\'s promptType.  `workflow-defined` is a wire-level sentinel. The CLI\'s message-service edge resolves it into a concrete prompt type before any runtime code runs: `sendMessage` (and the coalesced-drain path) calls the platform\'s `getConversationPromptType` endpoint with the run\'s `conversationId` and substitutes the returned value in place of the sentinel. A null response — meaning the conversation has no active workflow node with a declared prompt type — is rejected with HTTP 400 before the request enters Turn construction. Consumers downstream of the edge (resolver, prepare, dispatch queue, Turn lifecycle) therefore never observe the sentinel; there is no fallback to the run\'s persisted value. The route\'s payload-required guard does not apply to this sentinel.  `plan_shaping_feature_sync_discuss` is a follow-up-only persona for chat turns sent while the conversation is parked on the plan-shaping feature-sync review gate. Like `coding_freeform`, it is never carried on a platform EXTERNAL_AGENT trigger: the message-service edge resolves the `workflow-defined` sentinel, sees a `FEATURE_SYNC_REVIEW` gate on the `getConversationPromptType` response, and synthesizes this prompt type locally with the gate\'s design doc, plan, and outstanding flagged items. Because it is CLI-synthesized and never API-returned, it is deliberately NOT added to the `PromptTypeResponseDto.promptType` mirror in workflows.yaml (which carries only API-returned prompt types) — the lockstep rule between that mirror and this enum does not apply to it. The turn has no submission tools, so it cannot post a workflow completion.  `project_assistant` is an internal-only subagent promptType dispatched once per coding Run before the parent agent\'s first turn. It runs the Project Assistant handler as a subagent, emits a `CODING_RESEARCH` report, and records a `previousProviderSession` that is prepended to the parent\'s first prompt so the coding agent begins with relevant entity context already surfaced. It is never delivered via the platform API and has no `RunCreatePayload` entry. The `PromptTypeResponseDto` mirror in workflows.yaml is intentionally NOT extended with it.
 * @export
 * @enum {string}
 */

